= Module 3 — Selecting and Configuring Network Components; Implementing Network Security
**Weeks 5–7 · Lessons 17–26 (+ Evaluation and Remediation No 1)**
**Category of action:** Selection and configuration of network components; implementation of network security.
**Context:** Learners identify network equipment (17), sit Evaluation No 1, then set up and test a peer-to-peer network (18), plan equipment for an organisation (19) and integrate (20). Security follows: intranets and extranets (21), VPNs and privacy (22), computer and network security (23), data protection and access control (24), data validation and verification (25), and Integration Activity (26).

# Lesson 17: Network Equipment

> OBJ:
  - Explain the role of key network components (modem, hub, router, NOS…).
  - Differentiate between the various network components based on their functions.
  - Identify the appropriate network components required for a computer network.

## Lesson outline (2 periods, 100 min – equipment on display)

| Phase | Time | Teacher activity | Learner activity |
| Introduction | 5 min | Pass round a NIC, patch cable, switch, old hub, router, modem/ONT, access point. | Handle; guess roles. |
| Core 1 | 45 min | Components and roles: NIC, transmission media/connectors (RJ-45, fibre SC/LC), **hub**, **switch** (MAC table, full duplex, managed/unmanaged, PoE), **bridge**, **router** (IP routing, NAT, DHCP, firewall), **modem** (DSL/cable/fibre ONT/4G), **access point**, wireless router combo, **repeater/extender**, **gateway**, **firewall** appliance, **server**, **NOS** (Windows Server, Linux, roles), patch panel, UPS; layer of operation. Demo a switch's MAC learning with ping and ARP. | Component fact cards. |
| Core 2 | 25 min | Differentiation: hub vs switch vs router; modem vs router; AP vs router; repeater vs bridge; managed vs unmanaged; NOS vs desktop OS; comparison by function, OSI layer, addressing used, collision/broadcast domains, intelligence. | Comparison table. |
| Core 3 | 20 min | Identifying components for a given network: home; 20-PC lab; two-block school; branch office – list, quantity, justification. | Component lists. |
| Conclusion | 5 min | Exit ticket. | Answer. |

## Core content

### 17.1 Key network components and their roles

| Component | Role | Key facts |
| **Network interface card (NIC)** | Connects a device to the medium; frames data; has a unique **MAC address** (48-bit, e.g. 3C:52:82:1A:9F:07) | Wired (Ethernet RJ-45) or wireless (Wi-Fi); onboard in most devices; speed 100 Mbps/1 Gbps/2.5 Gbps; OSI layers 1–2 |
| **Transmission media & connectors** | Carry signals | UTP Cat 5e/6/6a with **RJ-45**; fibre (single/multi-mode, SC/LC connectors); coaxial; wireless (Lesson 30) |
| **Hub** | Multiport **repeater**: regenerates and **broadcasts** every bit to all ports; half duplex; one collision domain | Layer 1; obsolete – replaced by switches; useful for teaching/packet capture |
| **Switch** | Connects devices in a LAN; learns **MAC addresses per port** and forwards frames **only to the destination port**; full duplex; each port its own collision domain | Layer 2 (Layer 3 switches also route); **unmanaged** (plug-and-play) vs **managed** (VLANs, QoS, monitoring, port security); **PoE** powers APs/cameras/phones; 5–48 ports; uplink/SFP ports |
| **Bridge** | Connects two LAN segments, filtering by MAC address | Layer 2; today a two-port switch; wireless bridges link buildings |
| **Router** | Connects **different networks** (LAN ↔ LAN, LAN ↔ internet); forwards **packets by IP address** using a routing table; separates broadcast domains; typically provides **NAT**, **DHCP**, basic **firewall**, sometimes Wi-Fi and VPN | Layer 3; home "box" combines router + switch + AP + modem; enterprise routers (Cisco, MikroTik) |
| **Modem** | **Mo**dulator/**dem**odulator: converts digital data to the signal used by the access line and back | Types: DSL (phone line), cable (DOCSIS), **fibre ONT/ONU**, **4G/5G modem** (MiFi/dongle), satellite modem, dial-up (legacy); the interface to the ISP |
| **Access point (AP)** | Provides **Wi-Fi** access to a wired LAN; bridges wireless clients to Ethernet | Layer 2; SSID, channel, security (WPA2/WPA3); standalone or controller-managed; PoE-powered; not a router by itself |
| **Wireless router** | AP + router + switch (+ modem) in one box | Homes/small offices |
| **Repeater / range extender** | Regenerates/amplifies signals to extend distance (cable) or Wi-Fi coverage | Layer 1; Wi-Fi extenders halve throughput; mesh systems are smarter |
| **Gateway** | Device/software connecting networks using **different protocols** or acting as the default exit point (default gateway = router) | Protocol translation (VoIP gateway, IoT gateway, e-mail gateway) |
| **Firewall (appliance/software)** | Filters traffic by rules; protects the LAN from the internet | Layer 3–7; in routers, dedicated appliances (pfSense, Fortinet), host firewalls |
| **Server** | Provides services – file, print, web, mail, database, DHCP/DNS, authentication, LMS | Physical, virtual or cloud |
| **Network operating system (NOS)** | Software that **manages network resources**: user accounts and authentication, file/print sharing, permissions, services (DHCP, DNS, directory), security policies, monitoring | **Windows Server** (Active Directory), **Linux** distributions (Ubuntu Server, Debian, Red Hat) with Samba/LDAP, Novell NetWare (historic); also the firmware OS of switches/routers (Cisco IOS, RouterOS) |
| **Patch panel & structured cabling** | Terminates wall cables neatly; patch cords to switch | Racks, labelling, cable management |
| **UPS / surge protector** | Keeps equipment running and safe through outages/surges | Essential in Cameroon |
| **Network monitoring/management tools** | Observe traffic and faults | SNMP, ping/traceroute, Wireshark, controller software |

### 17.2 Differentiating components by function

| Comparison | Distinction |
| **Hub vs switch** | Hub broadcasts to all (layer 1, shared bandwidth, collisions, half duplex); switch forwards by MAC to one port (layer 2, dedicated bandwidth per port, full duplex, secure) |
| **Switch vs router** | Switch connects devices **within** one network using MAC addresses; router connects **different** networks using IP addresses, chooses paths, does NAT/firewall |
| **Router vs modem** | Modem converts signals to reach the ISP (one connection); router shares that connection among many devices and routes between networks; home boxes combine both |
| **Access point vs wireless router** | AP only adds Wi-Fi to an existing LAN (no routing/DHCP/NAT); wireless router adds routing, DHCP, NAT and usually a switch and modem |
| **Repeater vs bridge/switch** | Repeater regenerates signal without reading addresses (layer 1); bridge/switch reads MACs and filters (layer 2) |
| **Bridge vs router** | Bridge joins segments of the same network (MAC); router joins different networks (IP) |
| **Gateway vs router** | Router forwards between IP networks; gateway translates between different protocols/systems (broader term); "default gateway" in PC settings is the router |
| **Managed vs unmanaged switch** | Managed: configurable (VLANs, QoS, monitoring, port security, spanning tree); unmanaged: plug-and-play, cheaper |
| **NOS vs desktop OS** | NOS optimised for multi-user services, security, uptime, remote management, server roles; desktop OS for one user's applications (though it can share simply) |
| **Layer summary** | Layer 1: cable, NIC (physical), hub, repeater; Layer 2: switch, bridge, AP, NIC (MAC); Layer 3: router, layer-3 switch; Layer 3–7: firewall, gateway, proxy, servers |

### 17.3 Identifying the components for a network

| Network | Components required |
| **Home** (5–10 devices) | ISP modem/ONT or 4G modem; wireless router (with 4-port switch, AP, DHCP, NAT, firewall); NICs in devices; maybe a mesh extender; surge protector |
| **20-PC lab (star, P2P or client/server)** | 24-port switch (Gigabit, unmanaged or managed), Cat 6 patch cables/structured cabling + patch panel, NICs, router/firewall to ISP modem, one access point for staff, optional server + NOS (file/print/auth), network printer, UPS, rack |
| **Two-block school (tree)** | Per block: 24/48-port managed PoE switch, APs, cabling; core: managed switch with fibre SFP uplinks, fibre between blocks (or wireless bridge), router/firewall with content filtering, server(s) + NOS (AD/LDAP, DHCP, DNS, file/print, LMS or cloud), ISP modem/ONT (and 4G backup), UPS per rack, monitoring software |
| **Branch office linking to HQ** | Router with VPN and dual WAN (fibre + 4G), managed switch, AP, firewall rules, printer, UPS; VPN gateway/NOS at HQ |
| **Cybercafé** | Switch, router/firewall with bandwidth control (MikroTik), ISP modem, PCs with NICs, print server, UPS, timer/billing software |

> EXAMPLE:
  **Inside the school's "box".** The Camtel fibre ONT (modem) converts light to Ethernet; a MikroTik **router** does NAT/DHCP/firewall and splits learners/staff into VLANs; a 48-port PoE **switch** feeds the lab and powers three **access points** (SSIDs "Staff" and "Learners"); an Ubuntu **server** runs Samba/LDAP as the **NOS** for logins and files; a **UPS** keeps the rack alive during ENEO cuts; a **wireless bridge** links the boarding house 150 m away. When the internet drops, the LAN still works because switching and the NOS do not depend on the router's WAN.

> VISUAL:
  **Figure 17.1 – Component gallery**: photos/sketches with role, layer and addressing (MAC/IP) badges: NIC, hub, switch, router, modem/ONT, AP, repeater, gateway, firewall, server/NOS, patch panel, UPS. **Figure 17.2 – Data path**: PC → NIC → switch (MAC table lookup) → router (IP routing/NAT) → modem/ONT → ISP → internet, with the device that reads each address highlighted. **Figure 17.3 – Component checklist** for home / lab / campus / branch as a matrix of ticks.

> MISCONCEPTION:
  "The Wi-Fi is down so the router is broken." Often only the AP function or the ISP link is affected; wired LAN may work. "A switch and a hub are the same box with a different name." Switches learn addresses and forward selectively – a fundamental difference for performance and security.

> ACTIVITY:
  **Component identification lab (45 min).** (1) Station rotation: identify eight physical items, state role, layer, address type, one alternative. (2) Live demo: `arp -a`/`ip neigh` and switch MAC table (managed switch or Packet Tracer) to see MAC learning; `ipconfig`/`ip route` to see the default gateway. (3) Given three organisation cards, produce the component list with quantities, key specifications (ports, PoE, Gigabit, managed) and justification. (4) Compare quotes: hub-less star with unmanaged vs managed switch – which for a lab needing learner/staff separation?

## Formative assessment

> ASSESS:
  1. Explain the role of the modem, hub, switch, router, access point and network operating system.
  2. Differentiate hub from switch, switch from router, and modem from router.
  3. At which OSI layer do a repeater, a switch and a router operate, and which addresses do they use?
  4. What does a NOS do that a desktop OS does not? Name two NOS examples.
  5. Identify the components (with quantities) for a 30-PC lab with staff Wi-Fi and internet, and for a two-block school.
  6. Why is a UPS considered a network component in Cameroon?
  **Homework:** Photograph or sketch the network equipment at home/school/a business; label each device with role and layer; note any missing component you would add.

