# Evaluation No 1 and Remediation No 1 (Week 5)

> ASSESS:
  **Evaluation No 1 (2 periods).** Covers Lessons 1–17: AI concept, history, types and applications; AI ethics and responsible use; AI techniques and intelligent systems; machine learning and Teachable Machine; AI development stages, tools and Python; network fundamentals and stand-alone comparison; benefits, limitations, complexity and interoperability; network types by coverage and choosing for organisations; P2P vs client/server; physical topologies; network equipment.
  **Suggested structure (60 marks):** Part A – 15 MCQ/short-answer items (15 marks); Part B – structured questions (30 marks), e.g. (i) classify four AI systems by capability/functionality and discuss two ethical issues with responsible-AI principles; (ii) interpret a confusion matrix and explain overfitting; (iii) outline the AI development stages for a given problem and justify Python; (iv) classify given networks (PAN…WAN) and compare two on four criteria; (v) recommend architecture and topology for a described organisation with advantages/disadvantages; (vi) identify the component for each function in a network diagram and differentiate hub/switch/router; Part C – **practical (15 marks)**: build and evaluate a Teachable Machine model (or paper decision boundary) and label/identify physical components and a topology from a photo/lab walk.
  **Remediation No 1 (1–2 periods).** From item analysis: (1) AI types/ethics – card sorts and case debates; (2) ML principles – re-run the Teachable Machine bias experiment with guided reflection; (3) network classification and topology – string models and Packet Tracer/paper failure exercises; (4) equipment – station rotation with peer teaching; (5) individual feedback and a short re-test for learners below the pass mark before Lesson 18.

