# Lesson 18: Setting Up a Computer Network

> OBJ:
  - Demonstrate the steps involved in setting up a basic computer network.
  - Identify the essential equipment and how they are interconnected.
  - Set up and test a peer-to-peer computer network.

## Lesson outline (2 periods, 100 min – computer room)

| Phase | Time | Teacher activity | Learner activity |
| Introduction | 5 min | Show the goal: three PCs sharing a folder and a printer, pinging each other, in 60 minutes. | Predict steps. |
| Core 1 | 15 min | Steps overview: plan (purpose, devices, topology, addresses), gather equipment (switch/router, cables, NICs), physical connection (cable, link lights), configure addressing (DHCP or static IP – same subnet), name PCs/workgroup, enable network discovery and file/printer sharing, create shares and permissions, connect from other PCs, test (ping, access share, print), secure (passwords, firewall profile Private), document. Cable-making demo (T568B) if tools exist. | Sequence cards. |
| Core 2 | 60 min | Hands-on in groups of 3–4 PCs: connect to switch (or Wi-Fi router); set static IPs 192.168.10.x/24 (or DHCP); set workgroup "LABGROUP" and hostnames; share a folder with read/write permission for a local account; share a printer; from each PC: `ipconfig`/`ip a`, `ping` each other, open `\\PC-NAME\share`, map a drive, print; Ubuntu variant with Samba/Nautilus sharing; troubleshoot (firewall, subnet mismatch, cable). | Set up and test; record results. |
| Core 3 | 12 min | Testing protocol and documentation: checklist, IP table, results, faults found and fixed; extend: Wi-Fi P2P via a router; ad-hoc/hotspot P2P between laptops. | Complete lab sheet. |
| Conclusion | 8 min | Exit ticket. | Answer. |

## Core content

### 18.1 Steps involved in setting up a basic network

| Step | Actions | Notes |
| **1. Plan** | Define purpose (share files/printer/internet), number and location of devices, topology (star), wired/wireless, addressing scheme (e.g. 192.168.10.0/24, static or DHCP), names (PC-01…), workgroup name, who may access what | Sketch first |
| **2. Gather equipment** | Switch (or wireless router) with enough ports, Cat 5e/6 patch cables (or Wi-Fi adapters), PCs with NICs, printer (network or shared USB), power strip/UPS; optional crimping tool, RJ-45 plugs, tester | Check NIC drivers |
| **3. Physical connection** | Place switch centrally; connect each PC's NIC to a switch port with a straight-through cable; connect printer/router; power on; verify **link/activity LEDs** on NIC and switch | Cable ≤ 100 m; avoid running beside power lines |
| **4. Configure addressing** | Option A: router/DHCP assigns IPs automatically. Option B (switch only): static IPs – each PC unique address in the **same subnet** (192.168.10.1, .2, .3 …; mask 255.255.255.0); gateway only if a router exists | Windows: Settings ▸ Network ▸ Ethernet ▸ IP assignment ▸ Manual; Ubuntu: Settings ▸ Network ▸ IPv4 ▸ Manual |
| **5. Identify computers** | Unique **hostname**; same **workgroup** (Windows: System ▸ About ▸ Rename this PC (advanced) ▸ Change); Ubuntu: hostname + Samba workgroup | Restart when asked |
| **6. Enable sharing** | Windows: set network profile **Private**; Advanced sharing settings ▸ turn on network discovery and file and printer sharing; decide on password-protected sharing (create matching local accounts) | Ubuntu: install samba, share via folder Properties ▸ Local Network Share |
| **7. Create shares** | Right-click folder ▸ Properties ▸ Sharing ▸ Advanced Sharing ▸ Share this folder ▸ Permissions (Read / Change) + Security tab (NTFS); share the printer (Printer properties ▸ Sharing) | Use meaningful share names; least privilege |
| **8. Connect from other PCs** | File Explorer ▸ Network or `\\PC-01\Shared`; **Map network drive** (letter, reconnect at sign-in); Add printer ▸ network printer | Ubuntu: Files ▸ Other Locations ▸ `smb://pc-01/shared` |
| **9. Test** | `ipconfig /all` / `ip a`; **ping** each PC (and `ping` by name); access shares read/write; print a test page; measure a file-copy speed | Ping blocked? Allow ICMP or "File and Printer Sharing" in firewall |
| **10. Secure & document** | Strong passwords on accounts, Private profile with firewall on, remove Everyone/Guest access, antivirus; record IP table, share list, permissions, cable map | Keep documentation for maintenance |

### 18.2 Essential equipment and interconnection

| Equipment | Quantity (4-PC example) | Interconnection |
| Switch (5–8 ports) or wireless router | 1 | Centre of the star; each device to one port |
| Straight-through UTP patch cables (T568B both ends) | 4–5 | PC NIC ↔ switch port; printer ↔ switch; router ↔ switch (uplink) |
| PCs with Ethernet/Wi-Fi NICs | 4 | – |
| Printer | 1 | Network printer to switch, or USB to one PC and shared |
| Optional router/modem | 1 | Adds DHCP and internet; WAN port to modem, LAN port to switch |
| Power strip/UPS | 1 | Power for switch/router |
| Tools (optional) | – | Crimping tool, RJ-45 connectors, cable tester for making cables |

Wiring reminder: **T568B** pin order – white-orange, orange, white-green, blue, white-blue, green, white-brown, brown; **straight-through** (same both ends) for PC↔switch; **crossover** (T568A one end) for PC↔PC directly (modern NICs auto-MDI-X so straight works too).

### 18.3 Setting up and testing a peer-to-peer network – lab sheet

```
Group: ____   Switch port map: PC-01→1  PC-02→2  PC-03→3  PRN→4
IP plan (static, no router):  PC-01 192.168.10.1/24   PC-02 192.168.10.2/24   PC-03 192.168.10.3/24
Workgroup: LABGROUP     Local account on every PC: labuser / (strong password)
Shares: PC-01 \Shared (Read/Change for labuser)   Printer: HP-LAB on PC-02 (shared)
Tests:  [ ] link LEDs   [ ] ipconfig shows planned IP   [ ] ping 192.168.10.2   [ ] ping PC-03
        [ ] open \\PC-01\Shared and create a file from PC-03   [ ] map drive Z:   [ ] print test page
Faults found / fixed: ____________________________________________
```

Troubleshooting order: cable/LED → IP address and subnet (`ipconfig`) → firewall profile (Private) and discovery → credentials/permissions → name resolution (use IP instead of name) → services (Function Discovery, SMB) → antivirus blocking.

> EXAMPLE:
  **Three-PC P2P network for a secretariat in Kumba (no router).** An 8-port switch, three Cat 6 cables, static IPs 192.168.1.10–12, workgroup "SECRET". PC-A shares "Documents" (Change for account "staff") and the USB printer. PC-B and PC-C map Z: and add the printer. Pings succeed; a test file created from PC-C appears on PC-A. Fault met: PC-B could not open the share – its network profile was Public with discovery off; switching to Private fixed it. Total set-up time: 40 minutes; cost: switch + cables ≈ 25,000 XAF.

> VISUAL:
  **Figure 18.1 – Step ladder**: plan → equipment → connect → address → identify → sharing → shares → connect → test → secure/document, with icons. **Figure 18.2 – Star wiring diagram** of the lab exercise with port numbers, IPs and share names. **Figure 18.3 – Screens**: Windows IP settings dialog, Advanced sharing settings, folder Sharing tab, `ping` output, mapped drive; Ubuntu Samba share dialog. **Figure 18.4 – T568B colour order** on an RJ-45 plug.

> MISCONCEPTION:
  "If cables are connected, sharing works automatically." IP configuration, network profile, discovery and permissions all matter. "Ping fails so the network is broken." Windows firewall blocks ping by default on Public profiles – check the profile and rules.

> ACTIVITY:
  **P2P set-up race (60 min, groups of 3).** Complete the lab sheet; then swap two cables and a subnet mask deliberately for the next group to troubleshoot in five minutes. Extension: connect the group switch to the lab router, switch PCs to DHCP, verify internet + sharing still work; on laptops, create a hotspot on one and join from another (wireless P2P). No-equipment alternative: paper simulation with IP cards and "ping" tokens, plus a projected walkthrough.

## Formative assessment

> ASSESS:
  1. List in order the ten steps to set up a basic network and state the purpose of each.
  2. Identify the essential equipment for a four-PC peer-to-peer network and describe how they are interconnected.
  3. Why must PCs in a P2P LAN without a router be in the same subnet? Give a valid IP plan for five PCs.
  4. Describe how to share a folder with change permission and how another PC connects to it (Windows or Ubuntu).
  5. Which commands test connectivity and configuration? Interpret "Request timed out" vs "Destination host unreachable".
  6. Give three common faults when sharing fails and their fixes.
  **Homework:** Write a one-page illustrated guide "Set up a two-laptop network at home (cable or hotspot) and share a folder" suitable for a Form 4 learner.

