# Lesson 21: Intranet and Extranet

> OBJ:
  - Explain the concept of an intranet and an extranet.
  - Explain the technologies and requirements used to implement intranets and extranets.
  - Contrast the uses of intranets and extranets in organisations.

## Lesson outline (1 period, 50 min)

| Phase | Time | Teacher activity | Learner activity |
| Introduction | 4 min | Show the school's internal portal (or a mock) vs the public website vs a parents' results portal – who can access each? | Identify audiences. |
| Core 1 | 14 min | Concepts: internet (public) vs **intranet** (private, internal, internet technologies) vs **extranet** (controlled access for external partners); relation diagram; examples (staff portal, LMS, HR system; supplier ordering portal, parents' portal, bank–partner links). | Venn/diagram. |
| Core 2 | 16 min | Technologies and requirements: TCP/IP LAN/WAN, web/application servers (Apache/IIS, SharePoint, Moodle, Nextcloud, Odoo), databases, DNS internal, browsers/clients, authentication (directory/SSO, MFA), firewalls and DMZ, VPN or HTTPS/TLS gateways for extranet, access control policies, content management, e-mail/collaboration tools, bandwidth, security policies and legal agreements (NDAs, data protection). | Requirements checklist. |
| Core 3 | 12 min | Contrast uses: audience, purpose, access control, security level, examples, risks; case study of a school with both. | Contrast table. |
| Conclusion | 4 min | Exit ticket. | Answer. |

## Core content

### 21.1 Concepts

> DEF:
  An **intranet** is a **private network internal to an organisation** that uses **internet technologies** (TCP/IP, web servers, browsers, e-mail) to share information, applications and services **only with its members** (staff, learners). An **extranet** is a **controlled extension of an intranet** that grants **selected external users** (partners, suppliers, customers, parents) secure access to specific resources, typically over the internet with authentication and encryption.

| Network | Who can access | Where | Purpose | Examples |
| **Internet** | Anyone | Global public | Public information and services | School website, e-government sites |
| **Intranet** | Members only (staff/learners with accounts) | Inside the organisation (and via VPN) | Internal communication, documents, applications | Staff portal, LMS, HR/leave system, internal wiki, timetable, internal e-mail, shared drives via web |
| **Extranet** | Authorised external partners | Over the internet with secure login/VPN | Collaboration and services with outside parties | Parents' results/fees portal, supplier ordering system, bank–merchant portal, insurer–hospital claims, ministry–school data platform |

### 21.2 Technologies and requirements

| Layer | Intranet | Extranet (additional/particular) |
| **Network infrastructure** | LAN/CAN (switches, Wi-Fi), routers; internal IP plan; internal **DNS** (portal.school.local) | Internet connection with adequate bandwidth; public DNS name; static IP or cloud hosting |
| **Servers & platforms** | **Web/application servers** (Apache, Nginx, IIS), portals and CMS (SharePoint, WordPress, Drupal), **collaboration** (Nextcloud, Google Workspace, Microsoft 365), LMS (Moodle), ERP/MIS (Odoo, school MIS), databases (MySQL, PostgreSQL), e-mail server or hosted mail | Same platforms exposed selectively; often a separate portal/instance for partners; APIs for system-to-system exchange |
| **Clients** | Browsers, mobile apps on staff/learner devices | Partners' browsers/apps – must work on any device |
| **Authentication & authorisation** | Directory service (Active Directory/LDAP), **single sign-on**, roles and permissions, password policy | Separate partner accounts or federated identity, **MFA**, strict role-based access, time-limited access, audit logs |
| **Security** | Perimeter firewall, internal segmentation (VLANs), antivirus, HTTPS internally, backups | **DMZ** hosting the extranet server, reverse proxy/web application firewall, **HTTPS/TLS certificates**, **VPN** or zero-trust gateways, intrusion detection, data-loss prevention, penetration testing |
| **Content & governance** | Content owners, publishing workflow, search, document management, acceptable-use policy | Contracts/NDAs, service-level agreements, data-protection compliance (Law 2024/017 – consent, minimisation), terms of use, support desk |
| **Operations** | Administrators, monitoring, maintenance windows, training | Partner onboarding/offboarding, account lifecycle, incident response with partners |
| **Requirements summary** | Reliable LAN, server(s) or cloud, directory, web platform, skills, policy | Everything above + internet exposure hardening, legal agreements, MFA, DMZ/VPN, certificates |

### 21.3 Contrasting uses in organisations

| Aspect | Intranet | Extranet |
| **Audience** | Employees/learners | External stakeholders (parents, suppliers, customers, partner institutions, regulators) |
| **Primary uses** | Internal communication (announcements, newsletters), document and policy repository, HR self-service (leave, payslips), internal applications (timetable, marks entry), e-learning, knowledge base, collaboration | Order and inventory sharing with suppliers, customer self-service (tracking, statements), partner project spaces, parent access to results/fees/attendance, data exchange with ministries/banks/insurers, tender portals |
| **Access control** | Network location + login | Strong identity per external user, granular rights to specific resources, encryption in transit |
| **Security exposure** | Lower (behind firewall); insider risk | Higher (internet-facing); needs DMZ, MFA, monitoring |
| **Benefits** | Efficiency, consistent information, less paper, faster processes | Faster transactions with partners, transparency, reduced calls/visits, competitive service |
| **Risks** | Outdated content, over-sharing internally, single point of failure | Data breaches, account compromise, legal liability, dependency of partners on uptime |
| **School example** | Staff portal with schemes of work, marks entry, memos; learner LMS | Parents' portal (results, fees via MoMo), GCE Board registration link, supplier ordering for the canteen |
| **Business example** | Company wiki, HR system, internal chat | Distributor ordering portal, customer support portal, bank–merchant API |

> EXAMPLE:
  **Collège Saint-Michel's three webs.** Public **internet** site: admissions, news. **Intranet** (Nextcloud + Moodle on a school server, LDAP logins, reachable on campus Wi-Fi and by staff VPN): lesson plans, memos, marks entry, learner courses. **Extranet** (parents' portal hosted in the DMZ behind a reverse proxy with HTTPS and SMS one-time codes): each parent sees only their children's results, attendance and fee balance, and can pay via MoMo; the canteen supplier logs into a separate ordering page. Same platforms, three access rings.

> VISUAL:
  **Figure 21.1 – Three-ring diagram**: intranet core (staff/learners), extranet ring (parents/suppliers with padlocks), internet outer ring (public), with the firewall/DMZ boundaries drawn. **Figure 21.2 – Implementation architecture**: LAN with directory, portal, LMS servers; firewall; DMZ with extranet web server/reverse proxy; internet; partner laptops with VPN/HTTPS and MFA icons. **Figure 21.3 – Use-contrast table** rendered as two columns of icons.

> MISCONCEPTION:
  "An intranet is just the internal LAN." A LAN is the infrastructure; the intranet is the internal web-style information system running on it. "An extranet means giving partners VPN access to everything." It exposes only specific resources with strict roles.

> ACTIVITY:
  **Plan our intranet and extranet (35 min, groups).** (1) List ten internal information needs and match each to an intranet service; (2) identify three external stakeholder groups and specify what each may access, how they authenticate and which security controls apply; (3) draw the architecture (LAN, servers, firewall/DMZ, internet, VPN/HTTPS); (4) list the requirements (technical, human, legal) in a checklist; (5) optional demo: browse a Moodle/Nextcloud instance internally by IP, then discuss how to expose one page externally safely.

## Formative assessment

> ASSESS:
  1. Explain the concepts of intranet and extranet and how they differ from the internet.
  2. Describe six technologies/requirements for implementing an intranet.
  3. What additional requirements does an extranet impose, and why?
  4. Explain the role of a DMZ, HTTPS and MFA in an extranet.
  5. Contrast the uses of intranets and extranets in a school and in a business (two uses each).
  6. Give two risks of each and a mitigation.
  **Homework:** Design (one page) an intranet and an extranet for a hospital: services, users, access controls, technologies, and one data-protection obligation.

