# Lesson 24: Data Protection and Access Control

> OBJ:
  - Explain how encryption and access rights are used to protect computer data.
  - Describe methods used to control access to data and prevent unauthorised use.
  - Explain the importance of protecting data confidentiality, integrity and availability.

## Lesson outline (2 periods, 100 min – computer room)

| Phase | Time | Teacher activity | Learner activity |
| Introduction | 5 min | A USB stick "found" in the yard with fee records: who can read it? What if encrypted? What if file permissions? | Discuss. |
| Core 1 | 30 min | Encryption for data protection: symmetric/asymmetric recap, at rest (full-disk, file/folder, database/column, encrypted archives, phones), in transit, in use (emerging); key management (where keys live, recovery keys, HSM), password-protected documents, hashing passwords; demo BitLocker/VeraCrypt/7-Zip AES and LibreOffice password. Access rights: read/write/execute/modify/full control, owner/group/others (Linux), ACLs (NTFS), inheritance, deny rules, share vs NTFS, database roles, application roles. Demo permission set-up. | Practise encryption and permissions. |
| Core 2 | 35 min | Access-control methods: identification, authentication (factors), authorisation models (DAC, MAC, RBAC, ABAC), principle of least privilege, separation of duties, need-to-know, access-control lists, groups, directory services/SSO, time/location restrictions, network access control (VLANs, 802.1X, firewalls, VPN), physical access (locks, badges, biometrics), session controls (time-outs, screen lock), auditing/logging, DLP, data classification & labelling, secure disposal, preventing unauthorised use (account lockout, USB control, application whitelisting, watermarking, DRM, monitoring). | Design RBAC matrix. |
| Core 3 | 22 min | Importance of CIA for data: legal (Law 2024/017, banking, health), trust/reputation, financial loss, safety, continuity, personal harm; case studies; consequences of breaches for individuals and organisations. | Case debate. |
| Conclusion | 8 min | Exit ticket. | Answer. |

## Core content

### 24.1 Encryption and access rights for data protection

| Encryption use | How it protects data | Tools / examples |
| **Full-disk encryption** | Whole drive unreadable without key/password at boot; protects lost/stolen devices | BitLocker (Windows Pro), FileVault (macOS), LUKS (Linux), Android/iOS default encryption |
| **File/folder/container encryption** | Selected sensitive files encrypted; portable | VeraCrypt containers, 7-Zip AES-256 archives, GPG, EFS (NTFS), encrypted USB drives (BitLocker To Go) |
| **Document passwords** | Office/PDF files encrypted with a password | Word/LibreOffice "Save with password", PDF encryption |
| **Database/field encryption** | Sensitive columns (ID numbers, health data) encrypted; transparent data encryption | MySQL/PostgreSQL encryption, application-level |
| **Encryption in transit** | Data protected between systems | HTTPS/TLS, SFTP, VPN, WPA3, end-to-end messaging |
| **Password hashing** | Stored passwords cannot be recovered even by admins | bcrypt/Argon2 with salt |
| **Key management** | Encryption is only as safe as keys: strong passphrases, recovery keys stored offline/escrowed, rotation, hardware security modules/TPM, no keys in e-mail | Recovery key printed and kept in the safe |
| **Backups encryption** | Protects off-site/cloud copies | Encrypted backup software, client-side encryption for cloud |

| Access rights (permissions) | Meaning | Where set |
| **Read / Write / Execute** (Linux rwx for owner, group, others; e.g. `chmod 750`) | View / modify / run or enter directory | `chmod`, `chown`, file properties |
| **NTFS permissions** (Windows): Read, Read & Execute, List, Write, Modify, Full Control; **ACLs** with allow/deny; **inheritance** | Fine-grained control per user/group; deny overrides allow | Folder ▸ Properties ▸ Security ▸ Edit/Advanced |
| **Share permissions** (SMB): Read, Change, Full Control – combined with NTFS (most restrictive wins) | Control access over the network | Advanced Sharing ▸ Permissions |
| **Ownership** | Owner can change permissions | Take ownership (admins) |
| **Application/database roles** | Rights inside an app (e.g. teacher can enter marks for own classes only; bursar sees fees) | LMS, MIS, SQL `GRANT` |
| **Cloud sharing rights** | Viewer / commenter / editor; link vs named sharing; expiry | Google Drive, OneDrive |

Practice: create groups (Teachers, Bursary, Learners), assign folder rights to groups not individuals, remove "Everyone", apply least privilege, test as a low-privilege user, document.

### 24.2 Methods to control access and prevent unauthorised use

| Category | Methods |
| **Identification & authentication** | Unique user IDs; passwords/passphrases; **MFA** (OTP apps, hardware keys, SMS as last resort); biometrics; certificates/smart cards; SSO via directory (AD/LDAP); account lockout after failed attempts; CAPTCHA against bots |
| **Authorisation models** | **DAC** (owner decides – file permissions); **MAC** (system-enforced labels – military/government); **RBAC** (rights by role – most organisations); **ABAC** (rules on attributes – time, location, device); **least privilege**, **need-to-know**, **separation of duties** (the person entering fees is not the one approving refunds) |
| **Access-control lists & groups** | ACLs on files, folders, shares, databases, network devices; groups simplify management; periodic access reviews; disable/expire accounts (leavers, temporary staff) |
| **Network access control** | Firewalls, VLAN segmentation, **802.1X** port/Wi-Fi authentication, MAC filtering (weak), VPN for remote access, guest isolation, NAC posture checks (updated AV before joining) |
| **Physical access control** | Locked rooms and cabinets, badges/keys, biometric doors, visitor logs, CCTV, cable locks, disabling USB ports/boot from USB, BIOS passwords |
| **Session & device controls** | Automatic screen lock/time-outs, single session limits, remote wipe of phones/laptops (MDM), disk encryption, restricting removable media, application whitelisting, restricting admin rights |
| **Data classification & handling** | Labels (Public/Internal/Confidential/Restricted) driving controls; DLP to block sending restricted data outside; watermarking; DRM for documents/media; secure printing (release at printer) |
| **Monitoring & auditing** | Log logins, file access, permission changes; alerts on anomalies; regular audits; SIEM in larger organisations |
| **Preventing unauthorised use** | Acceptable-use policy with signed agreement; user training; usage quotas; content filtering; time-based access (lab accounts active only during class); licence management; incident reporting channel; sanctions under law (Law 2010/012) |
| **Secure disposal** | Wipe/degauss/shred media; revoke access and collect devices when staff leave |

### 24.3 Importance of protecting confidentiality, integrity and availability of data

| Property | Why it matters | Consequences of failure (examples) |
| **Confidentiality** | Personal privacy and dignity; legal duty (Law 2024/017 – personal data; medical secrecy; banking secrecy); trade secrets; safety (location of vulnerable people); exam security | Leaked learner data used for scams; exposed health status → stigma; leaked exam papers → cancelled exams; fines and prosecutions; loss of trust |
| **Integrity** | Decisions depend on correct data (marks, diagnoses, balances, contracts); legal validity of records; fairness | Altered marks → wrong promotions; tampered prescriptions → harm; modified invoices → fraud; corrupted backups → unrecoverable |
| **Availability** | Operations, services and safety depend on access (hospital records, results processing, mobile money, e-government) | Ransomware halts hospital; results delayed; revenue loss; emergency services blind; reputational damage |
| **For individuals** | Identity, finances, reputation, safety | Identity theft, extortion, discrimination |
| **For organisations** | Compliance, continuity, reputation, finances, competitiveness | Sanctions by the data-protection authority, lawsuits, closure |
| **For society** | Trust in digital services, elections, health systems, economy | Disinformation, exclusion, systemic failures |

> EXAMPLE:
  **The bursary share.** Before: folder "FEES" shared with Everyone/Full Control on the admin PC; a learner on the lab VLAN copied it, and a former clerk's account still worked. After: data classified "Restricted"; folder moved to the server with NTFS rights – Bursary group Modify, Principal Read, inheritance blocked, Everyone removed; share permission Change for Bursary only; folder on a BitLocker-encrypted volume with the recovery key in the safe; nightly encrypted backup; admin VLAN separated from lab by firewall; accounts of leavers disabled the same day by policy; access logged and reviewed monthly; clerks trained and signing the AUP. Confidentiality, integrity and availability all improved with no new hardware.

> VISUAL:
  **Figure 24.1 – Data protection layers**: data at rest (disk/file/database encryption icons), in transit (TLS/VPN), access rights (ACL table), backups – around a document icon. **Figure 24.2 – RBAC matrix**: roles (Principal, Teacher, Bursar, Learner, Parent, IT admin) × resources (marks, fees, timetable, personal data, system config) with R/W/–. **Figure 24.3 – Windows NTFS Security tab and Linux `ls -l` output** annotated. **Figure 24.4 – Consequences map** of CIA failures for a hospital, a school and a bank.

> MISCONCEPTION:
  "A password on the folder share is enough." Share permissions without NTFS/filesystem rights, encryption and account hygiene leave many paths open. "Encryption protects data from insiders." Authorised users with keys can still misuse data – access rights, logging and least privilege address insiders.

> ACTIVITY:
  **Protect the data (55 min, pairs, lab).** (1) Encrypt: create a VeraCrypt container or 7-Zip AES archive with a passphrase; password-protect a LibreOffice/Word document; enable BitLocker To Go on a USB (if available) and store the recovery key correctly; attempt to open each without the key. (2) Access rights: create users and groups; set NTFS/`chmod` permissions for a "Marks" folder (Teachers Modify, Learners none, Principal Read); test as each user; break inheritance; add a deny rule and observe precedence; compare share vs NTFS effective access. (3) Build an RBAC matrix for a school MIS and list three other access-control methods (network, physical, session) you would add. (4) Write a five-sentence justification of why CIA protection matters for the school, citing the 2024 data-protection law.

## Formative assessment

> ASSESS:
  1. Explain how encryption at rest and in transit protect data; name two tools for each.
  2. Explain access rights: distinguish Read, Modify and Full Control (NTFS) and rwx for owner/group/others (Linux).
  3. How do share permissions and NTFS permissions combine? Why remove "Everyone"?
  4. Describe six methods used to control access to data and prevent unauthorised use, covering authentication, authorisation, network, physical and monitoring controls.
  5. Define RBAC, least privilege and separation of duties with school examples.
  6. Explain the importance of protecting confidentiality, integrity and availability of data, with one consequence of failure for each in a hospital.
  **Homework:** Write a data-protection procedure (one page) for a class results file: classification, storage/encryption, access rights matrix, sharing method, backup, disposal, and the law that applies.

